From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 16:58:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] web-app: grab a poster when a video is attached
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Gives large videos a preview on their download-on-demand placeholder. Core
reads a `<blob>-preview.jpg` sidecar for that (see the core patch); this writes
it, because a browser already ships a decoder for every video it can play,
while core's wasm sits in a worker with no DOM.

- utils/videoPoster: `<video>` + canvas frame grab, seeking ~1s in so a fade-in
  doesn't yield a black frame, capped at 512px and 5s. Returns null on anything
  the browser can't decode, seek or paint — a poster is optional everywhere.
- useDraft: `addFileToDraft` writes the poster once the draft is saved, which is
  when the blobdir path exists. Gated on the guessed mime as well as the
  viewtype, since the composer stores pasted and dropped videos as `File` and
  core only corrects that at send time.
- runtime: optional `writeBlobPreview`, implemented only by targets that can
  write into the blobdir. Electron and tauri keep their renderer out of there,
  and their platforms have no pre-message placeholders to preview anyway.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

web-app: measure audio/video before sending so receivers see the length

Core measures images itself at send time, but it has no audio or video decoder,
and on wasm it runs in a Web Worker with no DOM — so `Param::Duration` was never
set on anything we send, `mimefactory` never wrote a `Chat-Duration` header, and
`PostMsgMetadata` carried `duration: None`. The visible result was our own
download-on-demand placeholder (desktop/0068) rendering its "–:––" fallback for
voice messages *we* had just recorded, and every other client showing nothing.
A video's pixel size is derived by nobody at all, so those placeholders could
not even reserve the right shape.

The browser already ships a demuxer for everything it can play, which is why the
poster grab (desktop/0069) lives here too.

- utils/mediaMetadata: `<video>` + `loadedmetadata` → pixel size and duration.
  One element type covers both media kinds — a `<video>` loads audio-only files
  fine, `videoWidth` simply stays 0. Every field is independently optional and
  an undecodable file yields `{}`: this enriches a message, it never gates one.
  Containers written by a live stream (MediaRecorder webm, and the header-less
  mp3 our own recorder produces) report `Infinity` until a seek past the end
  forces a scan, so that is done and bounded by the same abort/timeout as the
  rest. `once()` moves here from videoPoster, widened to `HTMLMediaElement`.
- useMessage: fill the new `MessageData` fields on the way into `send_msg`.
  This sits on `sendMessage`, not on the draft, because the composer does not
  send its draft — it builds a fresh `MessageData` from the draft state, so
  params written onto the draft never reach the wire. One hook therefore covers
  the composer, stickers, multi-file and drag-and-drop alike, with no draft
  state to keep in sync and invalidate when the attachment changes.
  Gated on the guessed mime rather than the viewtype, since pasted and dropped
  media travels as `File` until core corrects it from the suffix at send time,
  and skipped entirely when a caller already knows the duration.
  The three fields are widened locally, the way backend-com already widens
  `DownloadProgress`: this workspace typechecks against the published
  @deltachat/jsonrpc-client, which predates our core patch.

Images are left to core on purpose: it measures them after any re-encoding, so
doing it here would be a slower way to get the same numbers, or a wrong one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
